%I A401119 #25 Nov 01 2026 21:56:03
%S A401119 0,1,0,3,2,1,2,13,8,3,2,13,0,1,0,13,0,3,0
%N A401119 Number of orbits of exponents d, 1 <= d <= 2^n-2, under multiplication by 2 modulo 2^n-1, such that gcd(d, 2^n-1) = 1 and the power map x -> x^d on GF(2^n) has differential uniformity 4.
%C A401119 The differential uniformity of x^d on GF(2^n) is the maximum, over a != 0 and b in GF(2^n), of the number of solutions x of (x+a)^d + x^d = b. Equivalently, a(n) is the number of orbits of exponents d for which x^d is a permutation of GF(2^n) with differential uniformity 4. For n = 8 the only such orbit is that of d = 254, the inversion map x -> 1/x used in the AES S-box.
%C A401119 For even n, the known families of such exponents are listed in Table 2 of the Kölsch paper in the Links section.
%H A401119 Lukas Kölsch, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.12794">On the inverses of Kasami and Bracken-Leander exponents</a>, arXiv:2003.12794 [math.CO], 2020.
%F A401119 Conjecture: for even n >= 12, a(n) = 2*phi(n/2) + 1 if n == 2 (mod 4); a(n) = 3 if n == 4 (mod 8); a(n) = 1 if n == 0 (mod 8), where phi is A000010. This agrees with all terms for even n >= 12.
%e A401119 a(4) = 1: modulo 15, the orbit {7, 11, 13, 14} is the only one with gcd(d, 15) = 1 and differential uniformity 4.
%o A401119 (PARI) a(n) = {my(M=2^n-1, g=ffprimroot(ffgen(2^n, 'z)), X=concat([0*g], vector(M, i, g^i)), c=0, e, ok, v, mx, r, f); for(d=1, M-1, if(gcd(d, M)>1, next); e=d; ok=1; for(i=1, n-1, e=2*e%M; if(e<d, ok=0; break)); if(!ok, next); v=vecsort(vector(2^n, i, f=(X[i]+1)^d+X[i]^d; if(f==0, -1, fflog(f, g)))); mx=1; r=1; for(i=2, #v, if(v[i]==v[i-1], r++; mx=max(mx, r), r=1)); if(mx==4, c++)); c}
%Y A401119 Cf. A400802, A011260.
%K A401119 nonn,more,new
%O A401119 3,4
%A A401119 _Sajid Khan Hussain_, Oct 15 2026
%E A401119 a(19)-a(21) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Nov 01 2026
